meta {
  name: ORB Scan
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/orb-scan/backtest/ORCL?date=2026-10-13&rangeTimeframe=M15&scanTimeframe=M2
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  date: 2026-10-13
  rangeTimeframe: M15
  scanTimeframe: M2
  ~stSlopeTimeframe: M5
  ~emaSlopeEnabled: true
  ~emaPeriod: 20
  ~emaSlopeLookback: 3
  ~sessions: RTH,PRE,ATH
  ~count: 1200
  ~debug: true
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  # Opening-Range-Breakout — Backtest
  
  Replays a historical day for ONE ticker and reports exactly WHEN the ORB alerts
  (up/down) would have fired. Reuses the SAME live ORB logic (OrbSupport gates +
  SupertrendService + app.alerts.orb config) via OrbBacktester, so the output
  matches what fired live. Detection/analysis only — no orders, no notifications.
  
  An ORB signal = a CLOSE on the scan timeframe beyond the opening range (high/low
  of the day's 09:30 ET candle on the range timeframe), passing the configured
  gates (optional EMA-slope and Supertrend-slope confluence). Re-fires match live:
  a direction re-arms on any scan where it stops matching (price back inside the
  range OR a gate fails), then fires again on the next qualifying break.
  
  ## Path
  - `/{ticker}` — any US stock symbol.
  
  ## Query
  - `date`             — ET date, YYYY-MM-DD (default: today ET).
  - `rangeTimeframe`   — opening-range timeframe M5/M15/M30/M60
                         (default: configured orb.range-minutes → e.g. M15).
  - `scanTimeframe`    — breakout/alert timeframe M1/M5/M15/M30/M60
                         (default: configured orb.up.scan-minutes → e.g. M5).
  - `stSlopeTimeframe` — Supertrend-slope gate timeframe (default: configured
                         orb.st-slope-timeframe); only used when that gate is on.
  - `emaSlopeEnabled`  — override the EMA-slope confluence gate on/off
                         (default: configured orb.ema-slope-enabled).
  - `emaPeriod`        — override the EMA period for the slope gate
                         (default: configured orb.ema-period).
  - `emaSlopeLookback` — override the EMA-slope lookback in bars
                         (default: configured orb.ema-slope-lookback).
  - `sessions`         — RTH, PRE, ATH, OVN (default: configured).
  - `count`            — scan bars to fetch back from NOW. Auto-sized by default to
                         reach the date with EMA(period) warm-up. Broker caps a
                         single request at 1200 (M5), so very old dates may be out
                         of range — the response `note`/`warmup` shows coverage.
  - `debug`            — true to include `debugTrace`: a per-break-bar row with the
                         break level, EMA/ST gate results, fired flag, and reason.
  
  The two timeframe params are the "any range timeframe + any alert timeframe"
  controls — change them to explore different ORB configurations for the day.
  The emaSlope* params let you tune the EMA-slope confluence gate per request;
  when omitted, every gate on/off + lookback + threshold comes from app.alerts.orb
  (so the default run matches live exactly).
  
  ## Response (200)
  {
    ticker, date, rangeTimeframe, scanTimeframe, stSlopeTimeframe?,
    openingRange: { high, low },
    signalCount, orbUpCount, orbDownCount,
    signals: [ "09:45 ET  🟢 UP  @ 139.96  (break > 139.46)", ... ],
    gates: { requireBreakoutCandleColor, emaSlopeEnabled, emaPeriod, emaSlopeLookback, stSlopeConfirmEnabled },
    warmup: { scanBarsFetched, barsOnTargetDate, emaPeriod }
  }
  
  `signals` is a flat, chronological list of the breakouts that would have fired —
  each line is the ET time of the completed bar, direction + emoji, price, and the
  break level crossed. Signal times are ~1 scan-interval earlier than the live log
  (the live scanner fires on the NEXT scan after the bar completes).
  
  ## Debug trace (when debug=true)
  `debugTrace: [ { timeEt, close, attempted (UP|DOWN), broke, emaSlopeOk,
                   stSlopeOk, fired, reason } ]`
  One row per bar that attempted a break. `reason` says why it fired or was held
  ("deduped (still matching)", "EMA slope not rising", "ST slope not advancing up").
  
  ## Errors
  - `400` — invalid date/timeframe/session.
  - `502` — Webull market-data error.
  - `503` — no bars returned for the ticker.
}
